xnu 0181: power the GPU, authenticate the ZAP and start the CP

The GPU block needs its rails, clocks and the GMU before any register reads
back: GPUCC/GCC votes through RPMh, the GMU firmware over TCM/HFI, the GX
domain and its clock (gpupower/gmustart/gpugx). The probe ends with
`gpucc cx ... on`, `gmu hfi 0x1` and `probe done`.

The CP path (gpucp/gpusubmit): SMMU bank 0 takes SIDs 0/0x400/1/0x401 and
maps the SQE at 0x100000, the ring at 0x200000, the RPTR shadow at 0x300000
and the CP power-up list at 0x301000 with a 48-bit stage-1 context. The SQE
firmware (a730_sqe.fw, a generated local input) is copied and flushed; the
hardware init programs the GBIF, UCHE, NC_MODE, CP_PROTECT, APRIV and hwcg
sets. The ZAP is authenticated through PAS13, and the CP bases are published
again after it because the authentication resets the GPU. CP_RB_BASE and
CP_SQE_INSTR_BASE are 64-bit registers and need one 64-bit store: two 32-bit
stores leave the bootloader's 0xe000 in the high word. The PM4 headers are
real type-7 headers and CP_ME_INIT carries the power-up list.

What still does not work, measured: writing 1 to CP_SQE_CNTL never completes
and the next GPU access hangs, while writing 0 is harmless and the boot
reaches SpringBoard with the SQE stopped. ZAP before or after the start, no
ZAP, CP_PROTECT off, an AHB default response of OKAY, CP_ME_INIT without the
list, a software reset first, read-only or read-write SQE pages, write-back
or non-cacheable attributes and both SQE address windows were each tried and
do not change it. The a740 driver in drivers/ and mainline both run the same
sequence on their silicon, so the difference is this GPU's trusted state or a
start handshake that is still missing.
